[
    {
        "content": "<p>One of the next steps for multi tape Turing machines is adding combinators and of course many of them rely on basic data structures: Probably List.foldl and <a href=\"http://List.map\">List.map</a> are among the most useful ones but also much simpler ones like pairing and its projections.</p>\n<p>Since Turing machines operate on words, we need to encode these data structures and as we all know, already lists have maybe three different and equally popular ways to encode them.</p>\n<p>One way to fix this is to just choose one encoding and stick with it. I think Mathlib did this with pairing functions (although it’s maybe not really the same issue).</p>\n<p>Instead, I would prefer to stay flexible: I think the most basic types are Option, pair and List. We can assume encodings for them and require certain constructors and projections to be provided. From them we can then prove the complexity of List.foldl etc based on those encodings and constructors/projections. The main question here would then be: do we require „reasonable“ bounds for the constructors/projections or instead allow any and then derive the new bounds from them? The latter may make it over-flexible, and we can check by examples that the bunch of obvious encodings actually lie within the bounds.</p>\n<p>I can provide some more explicit code next week and would love to hear your thoughts on this!</p>",
        "id": 629076021,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Reitwiessner",
        "timestamp": 1791146132
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I can't speak to the TM side, but I've been thinking for a while it would be good to have a canonical way to say \"this &lt;model of computation&gt; computes this &lt;lean function / data&gt;\". That's maybe an orthogonal concern to choosing encodings, but perhaps something to keep in mind.</p>\n<p>in (now outdated) <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/pull/448\">cslib#448</a> i sketched an option which works well for combinatory logic, basically a typeclass packaging a relation <code>Realizes : SKI → ℕ → Prop</code> (for Nat, eg), which lets you lift to type constructors, say</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Realized</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SKI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Realized</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SKI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Realized</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SKI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Realized</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SKI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Realized</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SKI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 629165542,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Waring",
        "timestamp": 1791188466
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is what I have in mind for a pair encoding:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">PairEncoding</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">enc₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↪</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">enc₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↪</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">enc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↪</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- Pairing is a combinator: machines computing `f` and `g` from a common input, within common</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  bounds, combine into a machine computing `fun x =&gt; (f x, g x)` within the encoding's constant</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  factor of those bounds. Heterogeneous bounds are recovered by monotonicity:</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  `Turing.MultiTapeTM.PairEncoding.pair_mono`. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">pair</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">encIn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↪</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">},</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">ComputableInTimeAndSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">encIn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">enc₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">ComputableInTimeAndSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">encIn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">enc₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">ComputableInTimeAndSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">encIn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">enc</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- The first projection is computable in time linear in the encoded input and zero space. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">proj_fst</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ComputableInTimeAndSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">fst</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">enc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">enc₁</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">enc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">length</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- The second projection is computable in time linear in the encoded input and zero space. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">proj_snd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ComputableInTimeAndSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">snd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">enc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">enc₂</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">enc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">length</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 630430107,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Reitwiessner",
        "timestamp": 1791553089
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I have a couple of thoughts about this:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Maybe use separate time and space functions  for <code>fst</code> and <code>snd</code>?</li>\n<li>Can the space bound become <code>max (s₁ x) (s₂ x)</code>?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>I don't think it matters that much, should we optimize the constants in time and space usage?</p>",
        "id": 630549840,
        "sender_full_name": "Aviv Bar Natan",
        "timestamp": 1791585974
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think it's simpler with fewer bound functions and since s1, s2 &lt;= max s1 s2, it should be equivalent (i.e. the s in my version can be taken as max s1 s2)</p>",
        "id": 630551585,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Reitwiessner",
        "timestamp": 1791586830
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>my plan for this upper bound \"calculus\" is to aim for linear space and polynomial time and disregard any constant multiple. If we need more fine-grained results later, we can do this with fine-tuned specialized TMs.</p>",
        "id": 630551796,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Reitwiessner",
        "timestamp": 1791586919
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The main question is if we want to jump through such hoops or if we just want to fix one encoding, but I guess the only answer is: We have to try it out and see.</p>",
        "id": 630613035,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Reitwiessner",
        "timestamp": 1791624933
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't see an immediate reason that a fixed concise encoding will cause problems for some applications.<br>\nDo you have a specific use case in mind for two different encodings with different pros and cons?</p>",
        "id": 630616191,
        "sender_full_name": "Aviv Bar Natan",
        "timestamp": 1791627286
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I mean the argument for this is just the generic mathlib argument of \"be as generic as possible\", and I would have two very different kinds of encodings in mind: one doubles the size of the inner part (i.e. <code>true -&gt; false true</code>, <code>false -&gt; false false</code>) and then uses <code>true false</code> as separator. The other makes assumptions about the inner encoding, i.e. that it is a balanced-parentheses encoding and then the pair encoding would be <code>x, y =&gt; \"(\" ++ (enc x) ++ (enc y) ++ \")\"</code></p>",
        "id": 630617638,
        "sender_full_name": "Christian Reitwiessner",
        "timestamp": 1791628401
    }
]